<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="jack_daniels_bottle">
    <!-- Link origin sits at the center of the bottle's base, so placing the
         model's pose z at table height puts the bottle exactly on the surface. -->
    <link name="bottle_link">
      <inertial>
        <!-- 1.35kg: a full 750ml spirits bottle is ~600g of glass plus ~700g
             of liquid. It was 1.0kg, which is about what the bottle weighs
             EMPTY, and the bottles were reported as falling over too easily.

             Be clear about what this does and does not buy, because it is
             easy to expect the wrong thing. Mass does not change the angle
             the bottle tips at: that is purely geometric,
             atan(halfwidth / com height) = 17.5 deg here, and it is the same
             at any mass. What mass changes is the response to an IMPULSE,
             which is what a gripper pad grazing the bottle delivers - the
             angular velocity imparted goes as 1/I, so +35% mass is ~26% less
             rotation per knock. Nothing in this cycle pushes the bottle
             slowly, so that is the mechanism that matters.

             The weight is not free. The pour holds the bottle in shear at 97
             degrees, so 35% more mass needs 35% more friction; PAD_MU went
             1.2 -> 1.8 to pay for it, and the measurements are in
             bartender_description/scripts/render_bartender_urdf.py. If you
             change this number, re-read that table.

             Inertia is the same solid-cylinder proxy (r 0.0386, h 0.245) the
             previous numbers came from, rescaled - recompute both if the
             mass changes again, or the bottle will spin at a rate that does
             not match its weight. -->
        <pose>0 0 0.1225 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>1.35</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.007256</ixx>
          <iyy>0.007256</iyy>
          <izz>0.001006</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <visual name="bottle_visual">
        <!-- source mesh is Y-up (Blender OBJ export); rotate +90 deg about X
             so the bottle's height axis becomes Z-up as SDF expects. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.570796 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/bottle.obj</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.02 0.01 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.25 0.09 0.03 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
          <emissive>0 0 0 1</emissive>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Cheap primitive collisions instead of the 34k-face visual mesh, for
           stable, fast grasp physics.

           Three primitives, not one, because the bottle is not a uniform
           cylinder and the difference decides where it can be grasped.
           Profiling meshes/bottle.obj by height gives:

             z 0.000..0.149   straight body, 77.2mm across the FLATS and
                              103mm across the (rounded) corners - it is a
                              rounded square, not a circle
             z 0.150..0.170   shoulder, tapering 69mm -> 33mm
             z 0.170..0.245   neck, 33mm down to the 26mm mouth

           The body is a BOX rather than a cylinder on purpose. A 0.0386-radius
           cylinder matches the across-flats width but cuts the corners off,
           so the fingers would pass through where the real bottle still is;
           and it only ever gives the flat gripper pads line contact, which is
           a poor way to hold 1.35kg through a pour. The box gives the pads the
           flat faces they actually meet. It does mean the grasp has to be
           aligned with a face - bartender_pour approaches along +X and the
           bottle is spawned with zero yaw, so it is.

           The shoulder primitive is not decorative: it is the ledge directly
           above the pads that stops the bottle riding up out of the grip. -->
      <collision name="bottle_body_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.0750 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.0772 0.0772 0.150</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="bottle_shoulder_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.1625 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0250</radius>
            <length>0.025</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="bottle_neck_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2100 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0165</radius>
            <length>0.070</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <!-- Collar ring, for picking the bottle up BY THE NECK. The fingertip
           in ../../fingertip hooks its ledge under this, so the bottle hangs
           on a mechanical ledge instead of on pad friction.

           Real spirits bottles have this lip; the model did not, and without
           it a neck hook cannot work at all: going up the bottle the
           profile only ever narrowed, so there was nothing above the neck for
           a ledge to catch. r 0.0195 against the 0.0165 neck gives a 3mm
           radial overhang, which is what the ledge bears on. Sits just below
           the mouth, leaving 5mm of lip above and the neck bare below. -->
      <collision name="bottle_collar_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2360 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0210</radius>
            <length>0.008</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <!-- ============ Speed pourer ============
           A bar speed pourer (the tapered spout you hammer into a spirits
           bottle) rather than pouring from the bare lip. It does two things
           that matter here.

           It makes the pour controlled instead of a glug: a bare mouth
           alternates liquid and air and comes out in surges, which is why
           real bars fit these.

           And, more importantly for the robot, it moves the POUR POINT off
           the bottle's axis. The bare mouth sat on the axis, so every pour
           pose could be solved from the bottle's centreline. The spout tip
           is 17.1mm to the side and 54mm further along, and that
           point - not the mouth, not tool0 - is what has to be held over
           the glass. That is what the tool frames in
           bartender_teach/tool_frames.py exist to express: with the bottle
           grasped, TIP is the tool centre point, and a tilt rotates ABOUT it
           so it keeps its xyz while the bottle turns around it.

           Tip in bottle-local coordinates: (0.0171, 0, 0.2990).
           It leans toward local +X on purpose. The pour tilts the bottle
           about base Y, which maps bottle-local +X onto base -Z at the pour
           angle - so leaning that way is what points the spout DOWNWARD
           when the bottle is tilted, instead of uselessly sideways.

           Mass is left alone: a pourer is ~20g against a 1.35kg bottle,
           which is inside the rounding on that figure, and it raises the
           centre of mass by about 2mm. -->
      <visual name="bottle_pourer_body_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.2410 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.0095</radius><length>0.022</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.06 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="bottle_pourer_spout_visual">
        <pose>0.0086 0 0.2755 0 0.349066 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.005</radius><length>0.050</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.06 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <collision name="bottle_pourer_body_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2410 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.0095</radius><length>0.022</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
      </collision>
      <collision name="bottle_pourer_spout_collision">
        <pose>0.0086 0 0.2755 0 0.349066 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.005</radius><length>0.050</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
      </collision>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
